## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout enforcement**

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. This code uses it to kill a thread that exceeds a deadline — the fix is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with cooperative cancellation.

---

### ⚠️ Why the old code breaks

| Removed API | Runtime behavior in .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never fires — the exception type still exists but is never thrown |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

---

### ✅ Migrated code

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        // CancellationTokenSource is IDisposable — always wrap in using
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            // Task.Run schedules work on the thread pool instead of
            // manually creating a thread
            Task<T> task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

            // WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, giving us the timeout behavior
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Prefer this async overload in new code
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout,
        CancellationToken callerToken = default)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource
            .CreateLinkedTokenSource(callerToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token)
                             .WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!callerToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        // If callerToken was cancelled, OperationCanceledException
        // propagates naturally — don't convert it to TimeoutException
    }
}
```

---

### 🔑 Key differences

| Aspect | Old (.NET Framework) | New (.NET 8) |
|---|---|---|
| Cancellation model | Preemptive (`Thread.Abort`) | Cooperative (`CancellationToken`) |
| Thread management | Manual `new Thread()` | `Task.Run` on thread pool |
| Timeout mechanism | `thread.Join(timeout)` + `Abort` | `CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` + `WaitAsync` |
| Exception type | `ThreadAbortException` | `OperationCanceledException` |
| Cleanup | `ResetAbort()` | Not needed — cancellation is cooperative |

---

### ⚡ Critical pitfalls

1. **Work doesn't magically stop.** `Thread.Abort` forcibly killed the thread. With cooperative cancellation, `Task.Run(work)` keeps running in the background after timeout if `work` doesn't check a token. If `work` is CPU-bound and long-running, refactor it to accept a `CancellationToken`:

   ```csharp
   // Better: pass the token into the work itself
   public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
   {
       using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
       try
       {
           return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token)
                      .WaitAsync(cts.Token)
                      .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
       }
       catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
       {
           throw new TimeoutException(
               $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Don't forget to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It allocates a timer internally — leaking it leaks the timer.

3. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`.** Only catch it at the orchestration point (here, to convert it to `TimeoutException`). Let it propagate everywhere else.

4. **Truly uncooperative code** (e.g., a native call that blocks forever) cannot be cancelled with a token. Move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only .NET 8 equivalent of the old "force kill" semantics.

5. **`WaitAsync` is .NET 6+.** If you also target older runtimes, you'll need `Task.WhenAny(workTask, Task.Delay(timeout))` instead.
